WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:09.260
So if we think of the deep ocean
as being this dark,

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.620
deep, horrifying place
at the bottom of the Earth,

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:16.420
it's easy to dismiss it as something
which is out of sight and out of mind.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.180
But myself and colleagues have been there,

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.940
and I think if we take some time
to think about the deep ocean

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.140
as part of our planet,

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.220
we all might want to protect it
as much as any other marine environment.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:33.060
Let your imagination tell you
what a deep-sea animal looks like.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:37.100
The one that immediately flashes
into your head when you think deep sea.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.340
You're probably thinking
something like this.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:40.620
(Laughter)

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:41.820
And that is a real fish.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.700
That's a real animal from the deep sea.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.260
Now, that's the kind of image
you are quite often presented with

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.020
in the media when we talk
about deep-water animals.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.660
But there's two things wrong
with that photograph.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.340
First one, it's a little bit
misleading in that

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:58.620
those types of animals
are generally quite shallowish

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:00.100
in the grand scheme of things,

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.020
maybe a few hundred meters,
certainly top thousand meters.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.740
The second thing
that's a little bit misleading

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:09.300
is that they are normally about that big.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:10.540
(Laughter)

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:12.340
Right? So they're hardly that scary.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.900
(Laughter)

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.940
It's not going to come and get you, is it?

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:22.100
But we are forever being told
that there are "monsters of the deep"

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.420
in a low voice,

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.620
and aliens of the abyss.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:29.500
But when you take a moment
to think about the technicalities

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:32.140
of being an alien in the abyss,

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.700
the only alien in the deep sea
are the occasional human.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.980
Because everything else belongs there.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:42.060
So I am proud to say
I am an alien of the abyss.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.900
(Laughter)

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:47.700
And I've been studying the deepest
parts of the ocean for 20 years,

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.620
but more recently,
we've been diving in a submarine.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.340
One of the questions
you quite often get asked

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.260
when you tell someone,
"Oh, Tuesday, I went to 10,000 meters,"

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.020
they say, "Ooh, isn't that scary?"

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:03.060
And that is the important
point, it's fear.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.700
It's that thing that we have in our heads
that doesn't like being underwater.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.140
So if you think about what
the deep sea represents,

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.460
it's a physical,

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:13.780
three-dimensional manifestation
of two of the things we hate the most.

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:15.260
And we have a phrase for that.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.100
We call it the deepest, darkest fears.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.060
Because we are air-breathing mammals.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:23.020
So we don't really want to be underwater,
especially not super deep.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.780
And we're visually orientated
animals, we like light.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.980
But 11 kilometers underwater
is something that everyone goes,

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:32.340
"Ah, that's really deep and horrible.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:33.660
That's just frightening."

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.220
Take that 11 kilometers
and turn it on its side.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.140
I reckon most people in the room
have traveled 11 kilometers today.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.340
And the other thing
that we don't like is the unknown.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:48.940
And this is where we get
into this whole relationship

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:52.140
between reality
and what we're told on the TV.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.660
So on the TV, we're quite regularly told

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.940
that we know more
about the surface of the Moon

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.620
than we know about the deep sea.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.940
For starters, that quote goes back
before the Apollo missions

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:06.780
and a time where we knew not very much
about the Moon or the deep sea.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:09.180
So if you're on the surface
and you're looking out

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.060
and you can see all these lovely whales
and dolphins and squid and jellyfish,

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.980
all that stuff has to die.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:19.140
All that material,
all that lovely organic matter sinks.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.460
And where does it sink?

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:21.980
It sinks down to deeper depths.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.100
It ends up in the place we call the abyss.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:26.820
And not "the abyss" like they say on TV.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:33.100
The abyssal zone technically are depths
between 3,000 and 6,000 meters.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.980
OK, relatively shallow in my game.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:40.380
All that lovely organic material
comes down and settles there.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.660
And it gets eaten by deep-sea animals.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:46.420
Those deep-sea animals are eating it,
they're recycling it, they're burning it.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:48.460
They’re incorporating into the food chain,

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:50.660
and they incorporate it
into the sediments.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.180
So these animals are essentially gardening

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.460
because if they didn't do that,

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.580
these big, vast abyssal
plains of the planet

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:59.980
would become big,
stagnant, horrible cesspits.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:05.540
So the deep-sea animals are basically
irrigating a big chunk of the planet.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:07.220
And I say a big chunk.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:12.380
The abyssal zones account
for about 70 percent of the planet.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.380
Let's take a bit of a journey,
a descent into the deep sea.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.180
How do we know where to dive?

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:20.100
How do we know where we are ever?

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:22.020
Because we're always being told ...

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.500
(Laughter)

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:26.940
No, it's true, we're always being told
that we haven't mapped the oceans.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.900
A certain amount of seafloor
has been seen with the naked eye.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.340
I took that with my phone.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:32.580
(Laughter)

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:34.740
So I was seeing it at the same time.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.220
So it still counts.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:39.780
And there's a certain percentage
which has been mapped

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.620
or surveyed by remote systems.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:45.740
So this is about 2,000 meters deep.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:48.540
And about 20 percent of the planet

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.700
has been mapped in pretty high
resolution using acoustics.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:54.700
This is a huge, big trench
running off north of New Zealand.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.220
Well, basically they have been mapped.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:00.100
It all depends on what resolution.

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.020
OK so there's some places where we know

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:04.620
where absolutely every little
nook and cranny might be.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.220
There are no more
Mariana Trenches to be found.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.740
Imagine we're in our submarine,

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:11.620
this is us diving in the submarine,
see, it's very spacious.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:13.500
It has all the latest mod cons,

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.060
the sunlight diminishes very,
very quickly in the top,

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:17.700
maybe in the first two minutes.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:21.340
It goes that fast and you see
that it does get dark very quickly.

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.180
And as we descend down
through the water column,

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:26.940
we're always being told
that the water is very, very cold.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.220
Very, very icy cold water.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:29.940
And yeah, it's cold.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.060
It's not anywhere near as cold as most,

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:36.740
if not all of the winters I've ever
endured until moving to Australia.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.260
(Laughter)

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:40.900
It's about between one
and two degrees at deepest point.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:42.100
It's not overly cold.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.540
But those water masses
deep down are still moving,

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:47.180
they're still ventilating,
they're still full of oxygen

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.340
and they're moving heat.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:51.540
They're pulling heat down
from the atmosphere and the surface waters

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:53.340
and dissipating it around the planet.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:55.740
And as we go down the bottom
of the Mariana Trench,

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:59.100
we now have one ton
per square centimeter of pressure

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.060
squeezing down on that
titanium ball we’re sat in.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:05.940
Again, it takes a certain person
of a certain disposition to do this stuff,

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:09.100
but you can’t feel it on the inside,
it’s fine, it’s cold outside.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.260
And we’re moving around,

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:13.100
and if you look out the window,
we're looking mostly at mud.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:16.300
Imagine all that stuff
that has come down from the surface.

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:17.820
All that lovely organic matter.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.300
It's carbon, it's taking carbon
from the atmosphere,

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:21.980
it's being absorbed into the ocean.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:25.140
The surface of oceans basically comes
down and sinks into the mud.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:26.860
What happens to the carbon?

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:30.060
The bottom of the trenches,
the reason why the trenches are so deep,

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.500
the reason why they're
11 kilometers in some places

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:35.540
is because two tectonic plates
hit each other.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:39.300
Sometimes they get pulled apart,
sometimes they slide side by side.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:42.540
But the trenches are formed
when one tectonic plate hits another one

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:44.940
and drives it down
into the Earth’s mantle.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:47.980
Hence you end up with something
like Mariana Trench.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.020
Now all that carbon
trapped in the sediment

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:52.980
is now being pushed back
into the Earth’s mantle.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:56.500
The deepest points on the planet
are one of the few places

00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:58.780
where we're actually disposing of carbon.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:02.140
So they're again, performing
a service to the planet.

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:05.780
So when looking out the window,
what's it look like?

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:11.780
(Laughter)

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:13.860
If you switch your lights on,

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:15.620
right, it looks like mud.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:17.900
You have a lot of places
that are very flat.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:19.700
Lovely, luscious, golden-brown.

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:21.740
That's a good, healthy sea floor, OK.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:24.300
Sometimes you see some rocks
and some rocky outcrops.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.420
You see cobbles and rocks and boulders.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:27.780
Sometimes it's a bit crazy.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:31.220
But none of these images look that weird.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:33.500
They look like something
you would expect to see

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:35.140
if you were scuba diving at night.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.620
So the actual visual landscape
of the deepest places

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:38.900
isn't that strange either,

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:41.740
it's certainly not something
you should fear.

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:44.820
So let's get back
to the monsters of the deep.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:49.900
What do you reckon the deepest
tentacled animal is in the world?

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:51.460
Bear in mind, it's the tentacles

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:56.260
that have been putting the fear of God
into sailors since I don't know when.

00:07:56.780 --> 00:07:59.900
Imagine “The Thing” coming up
from the deep sea.

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:03.100
So you're probably thinking
something a little bit like that.

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:04.580
(Laughter)

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:06.940
That's also a picture I took
on my phone recently.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:08.100
(Laughter)

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:09.940
It's been a funny old week.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:11.620
That's ...

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:15.260
That’s the kind of image
your imagination puts into your head

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:17.820
when you think deep sea, tentacles
and the rest of it.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.380
The reality of the matter is --

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:23.620
and I can proudly say that I discovered
the deepest octopus in the world --

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:25.980
Audience: Aw.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:27.940
AJ: It’s the size of a puppy, too.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:29.420
(Laughter)

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:31.060
It gets better.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:33.620
Its real name is Grimpoteuthis,
which is a bit “rurr.”

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:36.820
But its common name is Dumbo Octopus

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:39.340
because it has big Dumbo-like ears.

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:43.980
And this lives at close to 7,000
metres in the Indian Ocean.

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:47.900
That is your big, scary octopus
that's coming to get you.

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:51.420
So then we think about what's
the deepest fish look like.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:54.020
Because they've got big,
horrible fangs, right?

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:56.660
And they're lurking in the darkness,
coming to get you.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:59.620
Deepest fish in the world
is actually what we call a snailfish.

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:01.940
If anything, it's a bit goofy-looking.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:03.620
It's not really that threatening.

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:05.740
It's not something you should fear.

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:07.020
They're snuffling around,

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:09.580
they're looking for stuff
and they've got little eyes,

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:10.940
they're actually gelatinous,

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:13.460
that's its liver you can see
on the side of its body.

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:14.660
They're very soft-bodied.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:17.460
And what I really love
about the deepest fish in the world

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:19.180
is they’re not really deep-sea fish.

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:22.180
They're actually a shallow water family
that has just radiated.

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:24.220
Most snailfish
are actually really shallow,

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:25.740
you even get some in estuaries.

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:29.260
It's just a fish that doesn't care,
you can't pigeonhole the snailfish.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:31.420
So ...

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.620
Another animal ... that we
like are prawns, right?

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:37.140
Everyone loves to eat a prawn.

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:40.060
That there is the last, the deepest
of the big crustaceans.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:43.380
That's a prawn, and those
are down to nearly 8,000 metres.

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:46.980
And that wouldn't look
that out of place in a fish market.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:48.820
There's nothing weird about it either.

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:52.060
And one of my personal favorites
from the very, very, deepest points,

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:55.620
and we see these bizarrely at the very
deepest points in multiple trenches,

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:59.260
we very rarely see them anywhere else
except for the most extreme places.

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:01.380
And it's a type of anemone.

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:06.060
And they just kind of look
like white flowers floating in the wind.

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:08.420
They’re actually really beautiful animals,

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:10.180
and they’re living in, unbelievably,

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:12.700
what we would class as being
extreme environments.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:15.780
They have an unpronounceable Latin name,

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:19.420
which I maybe shouldn't try,
but it's Galatheanthemum.

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:20.580
I've done it.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:24.100
(Laughter and applause)

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:25.260
Thank you.

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:27.220
(Applause)

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:29.900
I'm not doing it again, though,
I got away with it once.

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:33.100
So if you start to rethink
the visual imagery

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:36.020
and the way in which
the deep sea is in reality

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:37.660
and not the way we're told,

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:40.580
it's time that we started thinking
about the oceans generally

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:43.420
as just one big ocean,
from its surface to the bottom.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:46.300
The ocean doesn't recognize
imaginary lines that we've drawn

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.820
and said that's sea, that's deep sea.

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:50.380
So we care, don't care.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:52.180
Care, don't care, right?

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:54.300
And that's actually what we do.

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:58.260
You wouldn't turn up in the Amazon,
and say, "Right, everyone,

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:01.500
We're going to catalog every known species
for the first 200 meters,

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:03.860
everything after that
are monsters of the trees."

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:05.060
(Laughter)

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:08.700
"Because it's just dark and horrible
in there, so let's not do that."

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:11.100
But that is exactly what we do right now

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:13.260
to 70 percent of the planet.

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:15.500
And that's what I find quite frustrating.

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:18.980
Another question I get asked
sometimes as well is --

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:20.860
other than the fear stuff,

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:22.100
and the claustrophobia

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:24.300
and the toilet question
and stuff like that --

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:27.860
is, you know, what’s it like being
in the bottom of the sea,

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:29.780
looking out the window and seeing stuff?

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:33.380
And I actually find it a genuinely

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:35.260
difficult question to answer.

00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:38.020
Because it’s quite exciting.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:40.740
Afterwards, you’re like,
“Ooh, that was really exciting.”

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:42.500
But at the time, it’s quite humbling.

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:44.740
Because I think you’re kind of
aware of the fact

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:47.980
you’re seven miles underwater
and one ton per square centimeter

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:50.220
and all that kind of,
you do feel quite remote.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.700
But I just think it looks
kind of majestic.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:55.580
I think it's certainly
the exact polar opposite

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:57.900
of what you're told it should be like.

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:01.420
It’s not scary, it’s peaceful,
and it’s nice, and there’s beauty there.

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:05.700
And I think we need to challenge
the narratives that we are given

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:07.500
and change the story

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:10.700
and start to think of the deep sea
as a place which is fascinating,

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:12.780
it's wonderful, it's interesting,

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:14.460
and it's really, really important.

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:16.020
And only at that point,

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:18.900
if we take all that on board
will we ever truly,

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:22.660
truly protect and live
alongside our oceans.

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:26.140
Thank you.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:29.180
(Applause)

